Kipchoge won the [[2019 London Marathon]] in a time of 2:02:37, the second fastest marathon ever at that time, behind his 2018 Berlin Marathon win.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://results.virginmoneylondonmarathon.com/2019/?pid=leaderboard |title=Virgin Money London Marathon 2019: Results |website=Mika timing |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=28 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190428095450/https://results.virginmoneylondonmarathon.com/2019/?pid=leaderboard |url-status=dead }}</ref> He became the first man to win the event four times and set a new course record, beating his own 2016 London Marathon best by 28 seconds.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2019/04/28/sport/london-marathon-eliud-kipchoge-mo-farak-spt-intl/index.html |title=Eliud Kipchoge wins a record fourth London Marathon |author=Ben Church |date=28 April 2019 |publisher=CNN |access-date=28 April 2019 |archive-date=28 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190428140642/https://edition.cnn.com/2019/04/28/sport/london-marathon-eliud-kipchoge-mo-farak-spt-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The lead runner passed the half marathon mark in 1:01:37.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.letsrun.com/news/2019/04/eliud-kipchoge-runs-20237-shatters-london-course-record-to-win-10th-straight-marathon/ |title=Eliud Kipchoge Runs 2:02:37, Shatters London Course Record To Win 10th Straight Marathon |date=28 April 2019 |website=LetsRun.com |access-date=28 April 2019 |archive-date=28 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190428143839/https://www.letsrun.com/news/2019/04/eliud-kipchoge-runs-20237-shatters-london-course-record-to-win-10th-straight-marathon/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Mosinet Geremew]] (Ethiopia) finished as the runner up in 2:02:55 and [[Mule Wasihun]] (Ethiopia) came in third place in 2:03:16.<ref name="News" /> The British runner [[Mo Farah]], a four-time Olympic gold medalist and a pre-race favourite, finished 5th.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/28/sports/london-marathon-2019.html |title=London Marathon 2019: Eliud Kipchoge and Brigid Kosgei Dominate |last=Magra |first=Iliana |date=28 April 2019 |work=The New York Times |access-date=28 April 2019 |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=28 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190428170424/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/28/sports/london-marathon-2019.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
{{Wikinews|Eliud Kipchoge of Kenya runs marathon under two hours}}
In May 2019, a few days after his London Marathon win, Kipchoge announced another take on the sub-two-hour marathon, named the [[Ineos 1:59 Challenge]]. On 12 October 2019 in [[Vienna]]'s [[Prater]] park, he ran 4.4 laps of the Hauptallee in 1:59:40, becoming the first person in recorded history to break the two-hour barrier over a marathon distance.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ineos159challenge.com/news/spectators-guide/ |title=Spectators guide |date=23 July 2019 |work=[[Ineos]] 1:59 Challenge |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=26 August 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190826232158/https://www.ineos159challenge.com/news/spectators-guide/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.flotrack.org/articles/6479754-sub-two-part-two-eliud-kipchoge-to-take-another-shot-at-history |title=Sub-Two, Part Two: Kipchoge To Take Another Shot At History |website=flotrack.org |date=6 May 2019 |language=en |access-date=7 May 2019 |archive-date=7 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190507110102/https://www.flotrack.org/articles/6479754-sub-two-part-two-eliud-kipchoge-to-take-another-shot-at-history |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Britton |first1=Bianca |title=Kenya's Eliud Kipchoge smashes two-hour marathon barrier |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2019/10/12/sport/eliud-kipchoge-marathon-vienna-intl/index.html |website=CNN International |date=12 October 2019 |publisher=CNN |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=12 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012085158/https://edition.cnn.com/2019/10/12/sport/eliud-kipchoge-marathon-vienna-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
The effort did not count as a new world record under IAAF rules due to the setup of the challenge. Specifically, it was not an open event; Kipchoge was handed fluids by his support team throughout; the run featured a pace car and included rotating teams of other runners pacing Kipchoge in a formation designed to reduce wind resistance and maximise efficiency.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/2019/10/12/eliud-kipchoge-breaks-two-hour-marathon-record/ |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |title=Kenya's Eliud Kipchoge Just Became the First Person to Break the 2-Hour Barrier |author=Derek Hawkins |date=12 October 2019 |access-date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=12 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012124800/https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/2019/10/12/eliud-kipchoge-breaks-two-hour-marathon-record/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Agnew |first1=Mark |title=Eliud Kipchoge runs sub-two-hour marathon in 1:59:40, making history with first four-minute mile equivalent |url=https://www.scmp.com/sport/outdoor/trail-running/article/3032651/eliud-kipchoge-runs-sub-two-hour-marathon-15940-making |work=South China Morning Post |access-date=13 October 2019 |date=12 October 2019 |archive-date=12 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012114936/https://www.scmp.com/sport/outdoor/trail-running/article/3032651/eliud-kipchoge-runs-sub-two-hour-marathon-15940-making |url-status=live }}</ref> The achievement was recognised by [[Guinness World Records]] with the titles 'Fastest marathon distance (male)' and 'First marathon distance run under two hours'.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/595048-fastest-marathon-distance-male |title=Fastest marathon distance (male) |website=Guinness World Records |date=12 October 2019 |access-date=15 October 2019 |archive-date=10 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200810002349/https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/595048-fastest-marathon-distance-male |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/595080-first-marathon-distance-under-two-hours |title=First marathon distance run under two hours |website=Guinness World Records |date=12 October 2019 |access-date=15 October 2019 |archive-date=9 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210509052811/https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/595080-first-marathon-distance-under-two-hours |url-status=live }}</ref>
Kipchoge placed 8th in the [[2020 London Marathon]] in October with a time of 2:06:49, the lowest finish of his marathoning career so far.<ref name="runningmagazine.ca"/> The race was won by Ehtopias'S Shura Itata in a time of 2:05:41. Kipchoge lost contact with the lead runner with 3 miles to go. For the first time, the run was on a 19 laps looped course and originally set for April and postponed to October due to the Covid 19 pandemic.[https://worldathletics.org/news/report/london-marathon-2020-kitata-kosgei-kipchoge]
In preparation for the delayed [[Athletics at the 2020 Summer Olympics|2020 Tokyo Olympic Games]], he won the ''NN Mission Marathon'', which was held at [[Enschede Airport Twente]] in the Netherlands on 18 April 2021 in a time of 2:04:30. Jonathan Korir finished as the runner up with a personal best of 2:06:40.<ref>{{Cite web|date=18 April 2021|title=Athletics news – Rejuvenated Eliud Kipchoge wins NN Mission Marathon in Enschede, Netherlands|url=https://www.eurosport.com/athletics/tokyo-2020/2021/athletics-news-rejuvenated-eliud-kipchoge-wins-nn-mission-marathon-in-enschede-the-netherlands_sto8276988/story.shtml|access-date=18 April 2021|website=Eurosport|language=en|archive-date=20 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230420060020/https://www.eurosport.com/geoblocking.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref>
Kipchoge successfully defended his title from the Rio Olympics by winning the gold medal in the men's marathon at the Tokyo Games in a time of 2:08:38, becoming only the third person to successfully defend their gold medal in the men's marathon, after [[Abebe Bikila]] in 1960 and 1964, and [[Waldemar Cierpinski]] in 1976 and 1980.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Longman|first=Jeré|date=8 August 2021|title=Kipchoge finished far ahead of the pack to defend his men's Olympic marathon title.|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/07/sports/olympics/marathon-results-kipchoge-galen-rupp.html|access-date=8 August 2021|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=8 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210808010742/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/07/sports/olympics/marathon-results-kipchoge-galen-rupp.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He was the favourite to win and attacked around the 30&nbsp;km mark, looking back only once afterwards. He won by 80 seconds, the largest margin in 49 years.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=12 January 2022 |title=2021 LetsRun Awards |url=https://www.letsrun.com/news/2022/01/2021-letsrun-awards-best-athlete-best-comeback-best-wtf-performance-biggest-heartbreak-more/ |access-date=3 January 2023 |website=[[LetsRun.com]] |language=en |archive-date=3 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230103132745/https://www.letsrun.com/news/2022/01/2021-letsrun-awards-best-athlete-best-comeback-best-wtf-performance-biggest-heartbreak-more/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The silver medal went to [[Abdi Nageeye]] (Netherlands), while [[Bashir Abdi]] (Belgium) came in third for a bronze medal with 2:10:00. Kipchoge was the oldest Olympic marathon winner since [[Carlos Lopes]] won in 1984 at the age of 37. The run was staged 500 miles north of Tokyo in [[Sapporo]], with 106 runners participating.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Kenya's Eliud Kipchoge repeats as Olympic marathon champion|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Sports/kenyas-eliud-kipchoge-repeats-olympic-marathon-champion/story?id=79343991|access-date=8 August 2021|website=ABC News|language=en|archive-date=8 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210808155313/https://abcnews.go.com/Sports/kenyas-eliud-kipchoge-repeats-olympic-marathon-champion/story?id=79343991|url-status=live}}</ref> A documentary on the Ineos 1:59 Challenge, titled ''[[Kipchoge: The Last Milestone]]'', was released digitally [[video on demand|on-demand]] on 24 August 2021.
On 20 January, Kipchoge announced his desire to win all six [[World Marathon Majors]] (he had already won three, the London, Berlin, and Chicago marathons by that time). This was followed up by an announcement on 18 February that he would be participating in the [[2021 Tokyo Marathon]] (which took place on 6 March 2022 due to [[COVID-19]] restrictions in 2021) and that the majority of his recent training has been dedicated towards this goal.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Olympic champion Kipchoge to participate in Tokyo Marathon|url=https://www.reuters.com/lifestyle/sports/olympic-champion-kipchoge-participate-tokyo-marathon-2022-02-18/|access-date=18 February 2022|publisher=Reuters|date=18 February 2022|language=en|archive-date=18 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220218134350/https://www.reuters.com/lifestyle/sports/olympic-champion-kipchoge-participate-tokyo-marathon-2022-02-18/|url-status=live}}</ref> He won the Tokyo Marathon with a time of 2:02:40 – a course and all-comers' record. [[Amos Kipruto]] of Kenya finished second with a personal best of 2:03:13, and [[Tamirat Tola]] from Ethiopia came in third in a time of 2:04:14.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kipchoge and Kosgei race to Japanese all-comers' records in Tokyo |url=https://worldathletics.org/competitions/world-athletics-label-road-races/news/kipchoge-kosgei-all-comers-records-tokyo-marathon |access-date=6 March 2022 |website=[[World Athletics]] |archive-date=6 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220306050741/https://www.worldathletics.org/competitions/world-athletics-label-road-races/news/kipchoge-kosgei-all-comers-records-tokyo-marathon |url-status=live }}</ref>
