At the [[2023 Boston Marathon]], Kipchoge aimed to win his fifth of the six major marathons. However, after missing his water bottle and due to a left leg problem,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lorge Butler |first=Sarah |date=18 April 2023 |title=Eliud Kipchoge Says Left Leg Pain Plagued Him During Boston Marathon |url=https://www.runnersworld.com/news/a43633747/eliud-kipchoge-leg-pain-boston-marathon-2023/ |access-date=18 April 2023 |website=[[Runner's World]] |archive-date=20 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230420060020/https://www.runnersworld.com/news/a43633747/eliud-kipchoge-leg-pain-boston-marathon-2023/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cacciola |first=Scott |date=18 April 2023 |title=Eliud Kipchoge Was Supposed to Win the Boston Marathon. What Went Wrong? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/18/sports/eliud-kipchoge-boston-marathon.html |access-date=18 April 2023 |website=[[The New York Times]] |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=19 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230419010256/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/18/sports/eliud-kipchoge-boston-marathon.html/ |url-status=live }}</ref> the 38-year-old was unable to stay with the lead group on a hilly section after the 30&nbsp;km mark. He suffered the third defeat of his marathon career, finishing sixth with a time of 2:09:23. [[Evans Chebet]] was the winner in 2:05:54, successfully defending his title.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 April 2023 |title=Chebet retains Boston title while Obiri claims first major marathon victory |url=https://worldathletics.org/news/report/boston-marathon-2023-obiri-chebet |access-date=17 April 2023 |website=[[World Athletics]] |archive-date=20 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230420060024/https://worldathletics.org/news/report/boston-marathon-2023-obiri-chebet |url-status=live }}</ref>
At the [[2023 Berlin Marathon]], his first race since the Boston defeat, Kipchoge won the race for a record fifth time, finishing at 2:02:42. He ran alone from {{convert|32|km|abbr=on}} onward after Ethiopia's Derseh Kindie dropped away, but fell short of breaking his own world record that he had set the previous year. With the victory, Kipchoge became the first man in history to win the Berlin Marathon five times, having already won in 2015, 2017, 2018, and 2022.{{citation needed|date=October 2025}} Fellow Kenyan Vincent Kipkemoi came in third place in a time of 2:03:13. The half marathon split time was 1:00:22.[https://worldathletics.org/news/report/tigst-assefa-world-marathon-record-berlin-kipchoge-2023]
